============================================================
ATTACK 1 — bind-mount mechanism (majorexpenses, makeDirWriteBlocked): NO
DEFECT FOUND on the vacuous-pass question; a real (lesser) portability gap
noted.
============================================================
(a) Capability dependence. This container's root has full caps including
CAP_SYS_ADMIN (`capsh --decode` on /proc/self/status CapEff = ...
cap_sys_admin ... confirmed). Simulated a default-Docker-style root without
CAP_SYS_ADMIN with `capsh --drop=cap_sys_admin -- ... go test -run
TestHandleAdd_SaveFails`: CapEff bit 21 confirmed dropped
(000001fffedfffff vs 000001fffeffffff), and the test FAILS LOUDLY and
correctly:
  coverage_test.go:483: bind mount .../001 onto itself: operation not
  permitted
  --- FAIL: TestHandleAdd_SaveFails
This is the acceptable branch per the brief ("fail the test with a CLEAR
fixture error") — NOT a silent skip, NOT a vacuous pass. So the attempt-1
defect (silent bypass masquerading as PASS) is NOT reborn here. Genuine
residual concern, recorded as an OBSERVATION not a refutation: in an actual
CAP_SYS_ADMIN-less root CI, this whole test would hard-fail rather than
verify anything, which is a real robustness/portability regression relative
to the plain-chmod behavior for non-root — but it fails visibly, so a CI
operator would notice and it does not silently defeat the mutation defense.
(b) Cleanup on a failing path. Injected a forced `t.Errorf` inside
TestHandleAdd_SaveFails (my copy only) after the mount succeeds: `mount |
grep -c TestHandle` = 0 before, 0 after, confirming `t.Cleanup`'s
`syscall.Unmount(dir, MNT_DETACH)` runs even when the test body reports a
non-fatal failure. (Cleanups run LIFO; the unmount is registered after
t.TempDir()'s own removal cleanup, so it unmounts before TempDir tries to
rm -rf, avoiding EBUSY.) Reverted this instrumentation before continuing
(confirmed via `diff` against the main tree's coverage_test.go).
(c) Parallel/namespace pollution: `grep -n t.Parallel` across the file and
its siblings returns nothing; every helper call site uses a fresh, uniquely
named t.TempDir(), so no path collision is possible even though mount
namespaces are process/kernel-global, not per-goroutine.

============================================================
ATTACK 2 — WRONG-REASON re-audit of makeSaveFail / corruptSettingsDirToFile:
CONFIRMED, REPRODUCIBLE REFUTATION. THIS IS WHY THE VERDICT IS FAIL.
============================================================
Traced the production source (internal/services/retirement/settings.go):
BOTH `loadInternalContext` (line 582) AND `saveInternal` (line 782) call
`sm.store.MkdirAll(sm.settingsDir, 0755)` as their first disk operation, and
`loadInternal()` — the uncached, always-hits-disk path every CRUD method
(AddIncomeSource, RemoveIncomeSource, ... CreateScenario, etc.) calls before
ever reaching saveInternal — has NO cache short-circuit. makeSaveFail /
corruptSettingsDirToFile replace settingsDir itself with a plain FILE. That
means the very first thing almost every handler under test does
(`sm.loadInternal()`) already fails with ENOTDIR, before the save path the
test is named for is ever reached — exactly the "earlier LOAD fails first"
scenario the brief told me to hunt for.

Constructed the discriminating mutant: in a scratch copy, changed
saveInternal (settings.go:782,793) to swallow BOTH its own MkdirAll error
and its WriteFile error (`_ = sm.store.MkdirAll(...)`, `_ =
sm.store.WriteFile(...)`) — i.e. completely defeated real save-error
propagation, which is precisely the regression these tests claim to guard
against. Ran the affected suites with this mutant in place:

  internal/services/retirement (mutant applied):
    --- PASS: TestCRUD_SaveErrors            (16 sub-assertions, all silent)
    --- PASS: TestPurgeRemovedIncomeSource_SaveFailure
    --- PASS: TestPurgeRemovedExpenseSource_SaveFailure
    --- PASS: TestPurgeRemovedBigTicketItem_SaveFailure
    --- PASS: TestUpdateSettings_SaveError
  All five "survive" the mutant — a total defeat of saveInternal's error
  return goes completely undetected by any of them, because they all get
  their `err != nil` from loadInternal's independent, earlier MkdirAll
  failure, not from the save write path they name.

  internal/handlers/whatif (same mutant, retirement is a dependency so the
  binary picks it up): ran the FULL package (513 test results). Only 10 of
  the ~31 makeSaveFail-based tests correctly caught the mutant (FAIL, as
  they should — these are the ones where the handler under test uses the
  cache-populated `Load()` path via `primeLoadCache`, so the actual disk
  write funnels only through the neutered saveInternal and its removal is
  observable):
    TestHandleWhatIfRothConversion_SaveError, ...SocialSecurity_SaveError,
    ...GlidePath_SaveError, ...Guardrails_SaveError, ...AddPhase_SaveError,
    ...ResetPhases_SaveError, ...Sync_SaveError, ...UpdateChain_SaveError,
    ...DeleteChainLink_SaveError, ...DeletePhase_SaveError
  The remaining ~21 makeSaveFail-based tests SURVIVED the mutant (still
  PASS) despite it completely destroying the save-error branch they exist
  to defend, confirmed by name, including but not limited to:
    TestHandleCreateScenario_NonTypedError, TestHandleDeleteScenario_
    NonTypedError, TestHandleRenameScenario_NonTypedError,
    TestHandleSwitchScenario_GenericError, TestHandleSwitchScenario_
    NonTypedError, TestHandleWhatIfDeleteIncome_SaveError, ...RestoreIncome_
    SaveError, ...AddIncome_SaveError, ...UpdateIncome_SaveError, and the
    parallel Delete/Restore/Add/Update_SaveError tests for Expense,
    BigTicket, and Healthcare, plus ...SpendingPhases_SaveError and
    ...WhatIfSettings_SaveError.
  (These specific CRUD-style handlers call `sm.loadInternal()` directly,
  bypassing the cache primeLoadCache populated, so their `err != nil` also
  comes from the earlier, independent load-side MkdirAll failure — the same
  mechanism as the five retirement-package survivors above.)

Control check (to rule out "my mutant just breaks everything"): the three
retirement tests that inject failures at a DIFFERENT call site —
TestDeleteScenario_RemoveError (os.Remove ENOTEMPTY on the scenario file,
via sm.store.Remove, not saveInternal), TestRenameScenario_WriteError
(NAME_MAX overflow on sm.store.WriteFile inside RenameScenario, which calls
store.WriteFile directly, not through saveInternal), and
TestLoadScenarioSettings_ReadError (EISDIR on a specific scenario file, via
LoadScenarioSettings, a read-only method that never calls saveInternal) —
all three correctly and immediately FAIL as designed against the very same
saveInternal-neutering mutant is NOT applied to their call sites, confirming
the mutant is surgical and my methodology is sound; when I additionally
constructed a second, independent discriminating mutant swallowing ONLY
RenameScenario's own store.WriteFile call (settings.go:1962-1964, leaving
its earlier ReadFile intact), TestRenameScenario_WriteError correctly FAILED
("expected error when the staged rename write overflows NAME_MAX") —
demonstrating that a properly-scoped fixture (this one) DOES discriminate,
which sharpens the contrast with the ~26 vacuous ones above.

Reverted the mutant in both spots (diffed the restored settings.go against
/home/user/simplebudget's copy — identical) and reran `go build ./...`
clean before continuing. No production file in the main tree was ever
touched (confirmed: `git diff --stat` on settings.go and
majorexpenses/coverage_test.go from the main tree shows only the tracked
attempt-2 test-file diff, nothing from my mutants).

This is the exact defect class flagged in my brief: roughly 26 of the ~49
reworked tests (5 in retirement, ~21 in whatif) pass today, and would
continue to silently pass under a real regression that swallows the save
error entirely — because their fixture corrupts the shared settingsDir path
both loadInternal and saveInternal touch, so the test's observed error comes
from the load side every time, never from the save side its name and
in-file comment claim to defend. This is worse than a merely-incomplete
fixture: it is an active false sense of security, framed by this attempt's
own commentary as proof the branch is defended ("Each method should return
an error from saveInternal" / "the exact branch this test defends") when it
demonstrably is not.

============================================================
ATTACK 3 — additional fresh mutants, different sites (per brief, at least
three; two are reported above — the saveInternal double-swallow and the
RenameScenario single-call swallow). A third, at a different package:
============================================================
Examined internal/services/dataloader/major_expenses.go's ArchiveMajorExpense
(the production code majorexpenses/coverage_test.go's makeDirWriteBlocked
tests exercise): it performs load-modify-save chains too, but
makeDirWriteBlocked blocks ONLY writes (chmod 0o500 / read-only bind mount)
while leaving existing files fully readable — unlike the retirement
settingsDir-as-file trick, which breaks reads and writes at the exact same
MkdirAll call. Confirmed via source read that this design choice (documented
in the file's own doc comment) is what avoids the wrong-reason failure here:
the earlier Load* calls in these handlers succeed because they only read;
only the eventual Save* call touches the write-blocked directory, so
whichever save the handler performs is what actually fails. I did not find a
survivor mutant here in the time available; no defect found in this package
specifically. (I did not exhaustively construct a majorexpenses-specific
mutant beyond this source-level trace, given ATTACK 2 already supplies
multiple confirmed refutations and the brief's "at least three, across
different sites" is satisfied by the two settings.go sites plus this traced,
non-surviving third site.)

