============================================================
Distinguishing refutation from observation:
============================================================
REFUTATION (drives the FAIL): ATTACK 2. Roughly 26 of the ~49 reworked
tests (5 in internal/services/retirement, ~21 in internal/handlers/whatif)
do not exercise the save-error branch their names and doc comments claim to
defend; a mutant that completely defeats saveInternal's error propagation
is caught by NONE of them, because the shared makeSaveFail /
corruptSettingsDirToFile fixture corrupts settingsDir itself, which fails
loadInternal's own, earlier MkdirAll call first, on every affected call
path. This is a wrong-reason defect of the exact shape flagged in the task
brief, reproduced mechanically with named surviving tests and a working
counter-mutant, and it means "nothing lost defensive power" is false for
this attempt: these tests provide materially less defense than a mutation-
testing standard requires, for a defect class the ledger says attempt 2 was
specifically dispatched to fix ("The 30 whatif and 8 retirement failures are
expected to share fixture helpers — fix helpers, not call sites, where
possible" — RULING 2026-08-26a). The shared-helper fix path chosen
(makeSaveFail / corruptSettingsDirToFile corrupting settingsDir wholesale)
is exactly what introduced this cross-cutting wrong-reason regression across
both packages that share it.

OBSERVATIONS (not refutations, no defect found or out of scope):
ATTACK 1 (bind mount capability/cleanup/parallelism — clean, with one noted
portside portability caveat that fails loudly rather than vacuously),
ATTACK 3's majorexpenses third site (clean by design, traced not
mutant-proven), ATTACK 4 (NAME_MAX environment confirmed, race re-run not
repeated this attempt), ATTACK 5 (full module IS green both uids — the
literal criterion's mechanical bar is met), ATTACK 6 (no weakening, one
pre-existing out-of-scope root skip noted for the record).

Net: the literal "full module go test ./... green AS ROOT and as uid≠0"
bar attempt 1 failed on is now met. But the broader claim under
verification this attempt — "ALL of them are reworked with kernel-enforced
injections... nothing lost defensive power" — is false for a demonstrated,
reproducible ~26-test subset. Per my brief, I default to FAIL on this
finding since it is not ambiguous: I have working counter-mutants and named
survivors, not a hunch.
